[# Section 9 — assessing CX: the value map, served (RULED: DOCS-48, DOCS-47,
   DOCS-43, DOCS-49 wave 6, RS-30). docs-src/positioning/ring_value_map.md was
   an unserved positioning document in a business voice, each item carrying a
   `Backing:` pointer at a spec page — three of them archived. This page is
   that map in our own voice: the same items, one per claim, and beside each
   claim the conformance case this guide's `cx` replayed when the page was
   built ([fixture id=]) or the `make` step of the front door that holds it by
   name. A status tag is not typed anywhere: a claim with a replayed case is
   shipped by construction, and a claim held by a step is as true as that
   step's last run. Two items of the map are not on this page because
   neither a case nor a step backs them today — RESULTS.md names them.

   The nav puts this page at the head of the Repositories group: the
   assessment journey (DOCS4-DESIGN.md §1) lands here and leaves convinced
   from the front door's page and the contributor's front door. Cross-links
   use [[anchor]] form; a repository page is linked by its path. File
   extension .cxd — data-only source. #]

[section id=enterprise group='Repositories' n=9 title='Assessing CX'

  [intro
    """**TL;DR — we make structural claims, and we let you check each one.**
       CX is two rings and the groups above them: the data ring parses and
       addresses data and can execute nothing; the code ring adds programs,
       queries and authority; the platform group is the operating platform —
       storage, history, wire, live data — reached over the network and never
       embedded, which is what keeps the rings below it small enough to audit.
       Every claim on this page stands beside the conformance case this
       guide's `cx` replayed when the page was built, or names the step of
       the front door's build that holds it. If a claim ever stops holding,
       this page stops building."""]

  [body
    """The numbers on this page are counted, not typed: {{COUNT:modules}}
       shipped modules across {{COUNT:repositories}} repositories, each
       declared once in a registry the build holds the tree to, and
       {{COUNT:directives}} directives in the language. [[rings]] draws the
       picture from the same rows."""]

  [child id=enterprise-data n=9.1 title='The data ring — what a document is worth'

    [child id=ent-address n=9.1.1 title='One address per value'
      [body
        """Every value has one cryptographic address, computed from its
           canonical bytes, and the address of a value equals the address of
           the text that spells it — so reconciliation between two systems is
           the comparison of two hashes, not a project. Fewer integration
           engineers per partner, fewer disputes, faster onboarding."""]
      [fixture id=cx-025-hash-value-and-source-agree]]

    [child id=ent-same-meaning n=9.1.2 title='The same value everywhere'
      [body
        """A value is the same value whether it sits in a file, in a
           program, in a store or on the wire: what we write is what we
           read back, byte for byte, without a convention holding the two
           apart. The "it worked in system A and not in system B" incident
           class has no seam to live in."""]
      [fixture id=store-rt-001-round-trip-get]]

    [child id=ent-types n=9.1.3 title='Types without a registry'
      [body
        """Two parties agree on a data shape by exchanging one hash: a type
           binding names the value's address and its schema's address, and
           anyone can recompute and compare. A tampered value fails the
           check as a value, not as a policy. No shared server, nothing to
           host, nothing to trust."""]
      [fixture id=cx-113-type-binding-claim-shape]
      [fixture id=cx-115-type-binding-verify-tamper]]

    [child id=ent-exact n=9.1.4 title='Exact money math'
      [body
        """A fraction literal is an exact decimal, arithmetic keeps it one,
           and a decimal's scale and sign are part of its identity — `1.10`
           is not `1.1`, and neither is ever `1.0999…`. Mixing a decimal with
           a binary float is refused out loud, naming the one bridge between
           them. The scalar kinds are
           [`spec/core/cxdm.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md);
           the sign rule is decision 1353-b."""]
      [pair wrong=ap-decimal-float-mix-wrong right=ap-decimal-float-mix-right]]

    [child id=ent-no-execute n=9.1.5 title='A parser that cannot execute'
      [body
        """The data build of `cx` contains no evaluator: it parses, converts,
           canonicalizes, hashes, diffs and validates, and it can run
           nothing. That is verified at build time, byte for byte, by two
           standing steps of the front door — `make test-extraction-gate`,
           which builds the data-only artifact and probes it for every
           execution symbol, and `make test-profile-gate`, which grades each
           of the four builds against the corpus its surface promises.
           "Processing untrusted input cannot run code" is structural, not
           policy; [[rings]] says which build carries what."""]]

    [child id=ent-formats n=9.1.6 title='Every format, one model'
      [body
        """JSON, XML, YAML, TOML, CSV and the columnar binary form read and
           write through one value model, so one team keeps one pipeline
           instead of one per format. An XML document arrives as the same
           tree a CX document would; a JSON array is a sequence like any
           other."""]
      [fixture id=abi-010-xml-to-cx]
      [fixture id=json-006-parse-array]]

    [child id=ent-diff n=9.1.7 title='Semantic diff and patch'
      [body
        """A difference is computed on meaning, not on text lines: the diff
           of two documents is a record of what changed, and a patch applies
           it. Review over data — prices, configuration, contracts — becomes
           precise, and a change that changes nothing is empty."""]
      [fixture id=cx-041-diff-attr-record-shape]
      [fixture id=cx-042-patch-attr-and-add]]

    [child id=ent-schema n=9.1.8 title='Schemas that fail closed'
      [body
        """A schema states what a document must carry and refuses what it
           does not know: a missing required attribute is a violation with a
           code, a path and a message, and an unknown enumeration type or an
           unregistered name is refused rather than waved through. Bad data
           stops at the door, not at quarter close. The schema language is
           [`spec/core/schema.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/schema.md)."""]
      [fixture id=cx-136-validate-violation-item]
      [fixture id=validate-054-enum-values-unknown-type-fail-closed]]

    [child id=ent-longevity n=9.1.9 title='A fifty-year format'
      [body
        """The specification and its conformance corpus are the contract:
           every release ships the full corpus as its conformance bundle
           beside the binaries (the release flow's `package` step, and
           `make test-bundle-sources` holds the bundle's source list to the
           tree), so anyone can rebuild a conforming reader and grade it
           against the same cases we grade ours. The governance page's
           clean-room clause is the rule that keeps the spec implementable
           from its text and corpus alone
           ([`spec/process/governance.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx/blob/main/spec/03-approved/process/governance.md)
           §10.1a). No lock-in — including on our own implementation."""]]

    [child id=ent-columnar n=9.1.10 title='Fast bulk without a second system'
      [body
        """A typed columnar table is a CX element like any other — the same
           brackets, the same canonical bytes, the same address — and its
           binary wire form carries the same identities as the text. The
           corpus grades the table shape below and the chunked, compressed,
           schema-driven and Arrow forms of its wire (the `data_bin_*`
           suites of the data ring); [[analytics]] is the tour."""]
      [fixture id=tab-001-typed-columns]]]

  [child id=enterprise-code n=9.2 title='The code ring — what a computation is worth'

    [child id=ent-programs-data n=9.2.1 title='Programs are data with addresses'
      [body
        """Code, queries and configuration share the value model, so a
           program has an address like any document — the address of its
           quoted form is the address of the data document that spells it.
           You cannot approve, audit or price "exactly this computation"
           unless computations are addressable; here they are."""]
      [fixture id=cx-094-quote-hash-is-tier1]]

    [child id=ent-reproducible n=9.2.2 title='Reproducible computation, by construction'
      [body
        """A pure computation is deterministic — a property of the model,
           not a team discipline — and each carries an identity over its
           function, its inputs and its environment: two different
           computations never share one. "Show exactly what produced this
           number" is a lookup, not a forensic project."""]
      [fixture id=cx-108-computation-id-injectivity]]

    [child id=ent-effects n=9.2.3 title='Side effects are declared and enforced'
      [body
        """A command states what it touches, and the runtime holds it to the
           statement: a declared effect runs under its grant, an undeclared
           one is refused before anything happens. The blast radius is known
           before the program runs — incident prevention rather than
           response. The vocabulary is
           [`spec/core/commands_effects.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/commands_effects.md)."""]
      [fixture id=cmd-001-command-effects-declared-cap-allowed]
      [fixture id=cmd-002-command-effects-undeclared-cap-denied]]

    [child id=ent-delegation n=9.2.4 title='Authority is delegated explicitly and only narrows'
      [body
        """Authority is granted in writing and passed on only through a
           presented delegation; a grant row compiles to exactly one root
           delegation, and what a holder may do is what the chain permits
           and nothing wider. You can state, in writing, the most an
           automated caller can do."""]
      [fixture id=authz-091-basis-permits-through-the-presented-delegation]
      [fixture id=store-grants-001-row-compiles-to-one-root-delegation]]

    [child id=ent-propose n=9.2.5 title='Approve exactly the code that runs'
      [body
        """Propose mode binds a human approval to the address of the precise
           program text; a proposal is a value you can read, and a
           precondition that no longer holds refuses at commit. "Who
           approved this action, and what exactly did they approve?" has one
           answer."""]
      [fixture id=cmd-019-propose-value-shape]
      [fixture id=cmd-020-propose-precondition-false-refuses]]

    [child id=ent-idempotent n=9.2.6 title='Double-execution protection built in'
      [body
        """An idempotent command executes once inside its window however
           many times it is called — the key derived from the request
           itself when none is supplied — and a command that is not declared
           idempotent is never silently deduplicated. The double-refund and
           double-shipment defect class is removed at the platform, not
           per team."""]
      [fixture id=cmd-013-idempotent-direct-call-dedups]
      [fixture id=cmd-018-non-idempotent-never-dedups]]

    [child id=ent-plans n=9.2.7 title='One query tier that optimizes itself'
      [body
        """A query has a canonical plan with its own address, and two
           spellings of the same plan share one address — so they share one
           cache, and repeated analytical work is paid for once,
           organization-wide, with no coordination asked of anyone."""]
      [fixture id=cx-100-plan-address-byte-pin]
      [fixture id=cx-101-plan-pair-alpha-respelling]]

    [child id=ent-tools n=9.2.8 title='Agent tools derived, not maintained'
      [body
        """A command definition projects to an agent tool descriptor by
           itself — a definition with an effects clause is a command, and a
           command is a tool — and an agent-protocol request is an ordinary
           value projected to its wire form. New capabilities are
           agent-ready the day they ship, and there is no wrapper to drift.
           The projection is
           [`spec/x/agent_tool_projection.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-platform-agent/blob/main/spec/03-approved/x/agent_tool_projection.md)."""]
      [fixture id=tools-004-zero-item-effects-is-a-command]
      [fixture id=mcp-001-call-tool-request-shape]]

    [child id=ent-sandbox n=9.2.9 title='Run third-party logic safely'
      [body
        """Untrusted code evaluates in a sandbox that can only narrow the
           caller's library set — never widen it — under a recursion ceiling
           the runtime enforces. A partner or extension surface opens
           without opening a hole."""]
      [fixture id=cx-125-eval-lib-narrowing-allowed]
      [fixture id=cx-126-eval-lib-widen-refused]
      [fixture id=cx-127-eval-depth-exceeded]]]

  [child id=enterprise-platform n=9.3 title='The platform group — what the system of record is worth'

    [child id=ent-store n=9.3.1 title='A store that works like git for data'
      [body
        """Documents are content-addressed; named branches move with a
           visible log, a forced move is recorded as one, and a compare-
           and-set against a stale head refuses. "Who changed what, when,
           and to what" is intrinsic to the store, so the audit-trail line
           item leaves every application built on it. The store is
           [`spec/platform/store.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-platform-store/blob/main/spec/03-approved/platform/store.md)."""]
      [fixture id=store-log-003-branch-advances-appear]
      [fixture id=store-branch-002-force-moves]
      [fixture id=store-branch-003-cas-conflict]]

    [child id=ent-journal n=9.3.2 title='A tamper-evident system of record'
      [body
        """Journal entries are hash-chained, state is rebuilt by
           deterministic folds, and verification is a verb: an intact chain
           verifies to a value, and a tampered one answers a finding that
           names the break — also a value, never a crash. Prove the ledger
           to a regulator or a counterparty on demand."""]
      [fixture id=journal-019-verify-valid]
      [fixture id=journal-022-tamper-finding-is-value]]

    [child id=ent-time n=9.3.3 title='Time travel on business data'
      [body
        """Valid time and record time are separate, first-class axes: an
           entry's validity may be open-ended, and a correction is a new
           entry chained to the one it corrects, so "what was true on the
           third" and "what did we believe on the third" are both ordinary
           reads. Back-dated corrections and restatements become queries.
           The semantics are
           [`spec/core/bitemporal.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/bitemporal.md)."""]
      [fixture id=journal-111-open-ended-validity]
      [fixture id=journal-146-m5-correction-chain-depth-two]]

    [child id=ent-erasure n=9.3.4 title='Right-to-be-forgotten in an append-only world'
      [body
        """Erasure is a first-class entry: applying it purges the payload
           and the deduplicated records that carried it while every chain
           still verifies, because what the chain covers is the address, not
           the bytes we no longer hold. Compliance without giving up
           tamper-evident history — the combination regulated industries
           cannot otherwise buy. The design is
           [`spec/platform/store/erasure_compliance.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-platform-store/blob/main/spec/03-approved/platform/store/erasure_compliance.md)."""]
      [fixture id=journal-137-apply-erasures]
      [fixture id=journal-131-erase-purges-dedup-records]]

    [child id=ent-wire n=9.3.5 title='One security model on one wire'
      [body
        """Identity and permission are one system across queries, events
           and storage, and the connection's own negotiation rides inside
           the signed transcript: a replayed handshake message fails its
           signature, and a version the peer did not offer is refused
           rather than downgraded to. One security review instead of
           three."""]
      [fixture id=xsp-auth-004-replay-m2-fails-sig]
      [fixture id=xsp-auth-008-version-not-offered]]

    [child id=ent-live n=9.3.6 title='Live and batch are the same question'
      [body
        """A query runs once, stays maintained, or answers changes-since a
           cursor — the same text, and the answers are equal by contract,
           which the corpus grades leg by leg. Real-time dashboards and
           pipelines without a second streaming stack."""]
      [fixture id=live-002-quartet-leg1-for-equivalence]
      [fixture id=live-004-inserts-since-cursor]]

    [child id=ent-replicas n=9.3.7 title='Offline and multi-site by design'
      [body
        """A replica is seeded and ingests locally with no central sequencer,
           and a merge is itself an entry with recorded lineage — the one
           conflict value is how disagreement is spelled, and a resolution
           is an entry anyone can read later. Field, branch and edge
           scenarios work disconnected and reconcile honestly. The design is
           [`spec/platform/store/distributed_store.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-platform-store/blob/main/spec/03-approved/platform/store/distributed_store.md)."""]
      [fixture id=journal-117-offline-replica-seed]
      [fixture id=store-reconcile-003-resolution-merge-entry]]

    [child id=ent-flows n=9.3.8 title='Cross-system workflows that fail safely'
      [body
        """A workflow is a document; before its pivot a failed step is
           compensated in reverse order, after it the run retries forward,
           and the record says which. The half-committed transaction —
           the worst outage class in distributed systems — is designed
           out; what remains is visible and recoverable. The runner law is
           [`spec/platform/flow.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-platform-flow/blob/main/spec/03-approved/platform/flow.md)."""]
      [fixture id=flow-032-compensation-triple]
      [fixture id=flow-012-simulate-compensation-and-incomplete]]

    [child id=ent-evolution n=9.3.9 title='Data that survives schema change'
      [body
        """Old entries upgrade through declared upcasters at one seam, and
           an upcaster that cannot cover its input refuses instead of
           guessing, so history stays replayable across every format the
           schema has had. The recurring migration project becomes a
           declaration."""]
      [fixture id=journal-118-upcaster-seam]
      [fixture id=journal-120-upcast-refusals]]

    [child id=ent-messaging n=9.3.10 title='Messaging without a broker'
      [body
        """Fan-out channels with glob subscriptions, a delivery count you
           can read, and a dead-letter path for a record that breaks its
           bound — on the same store and the same security model as
           everything else. One vendor, one cluster and one pager rotation
           fewer, with a stronger audit story than the broker they replace.
           The unified model is
           [`spec/core/delivery.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/delivery.md)."""]
      [fixture id=fabric-004-subscribe-glob-receive]
      [fixture id=bus-005-delivered-count]
      [fixture id=sync-011-a-record-over-the-byte-bound-is-dead-lettered]]]

  [child id=enterprise-next n=9.4 title='Where to go next'
    [body
      """The front door's page, [cx](repo-cx.html), is where the claims above
         are assembled: the pins, the four builds of one binary, the release
         and the installer, each held by a step. The contributor's front door,
         [`llm/contributor.md`](https://cxhome.org/llm/contributor.md), lists
         every step of the build with the decision it holds. [[rings]] is the
         map of what you take on; [[quickstart]] runs a first file in five
         minutes; every repository in the group below has a page in the same
         shape — what it is, what it gives you, one replayed example, its
         status and its links."""]]]
